\id DAG - Brenton English Septuagint 
\h Daniel (Greek) 
\toc1 Daniel (Greek) 
\toc2 Daniel (Greek) 
\toc3 Dan. 
\mt1 DANIEL 
\c 1 
\p
\v 1 \sc In\sc* the third year of the reign of Joakim king of Judah, Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon came to Jerusalem, and besieged it. 
\v 2 The Lord gave Joakim king of Judah into his hand, and part of the vessels of the house of God: and he brought them into the land of Sennaar to the house of his god; and he brought the vessels into the treasure-house of his god. 
\v 3 The king told Asphanez his chief eunuch, to bring in \add some\add* of the captive children of Israel, and of the seed of the kingdom, and of the princes; 
\v 4 young men in whom was no blemish, and beautiful in appearance, and skilled in all wisdom, and possessing knowledge, and acquainted with prudence, and who had ability to stand in the house before the king, and \add the king gave commandment\add* to teach them the learning and language of the Chaldeans. 
\p
\v 5 The king appointed them a daily portion from the king's table, and from the wine which he drank; and \add gave orders\add* to nourish them three years, and \add that\add* afterwards they should stand before the king. 
\p
\v 6 Now these were among them of the children of Judah: Daniel, and Ananias, and Azariah, and Mishael. 
\v 7 The chief of the eunuchs gave them names: to Daniel, Belteshazzar; and to Ananias, Shadrach; and to Mishael, Meshach; and to Azariah, Abednego. 
\v 8 Daniel purposed in his heart, that he would not defile himself with the king's table, nor with the wine of his drink: and he asked the chief of the eunuchs that he might not defile himself. 
\v 9 Now God \add had\add* brought Daniel into favour and compassion with the chief of the eunuchs. 
\v 10 The chief of the eunuchs said to Daniel, I fear my lord the king, who has appointed your food and your drink, lest he see your countenances gloomy in comparison to the young men your equals; so you will endanger my head with the king. 
\v 11 Daniel said to Amelsad, whom the chief of the eunuchs had appointed over Daniel, Ananias, Mishael, \add and\add* Azariah, 
\v 12 Prove now your servants ten days; and let them give us \f + \fr 1:10 \fqa Gr. \ft seeds.\f*vegetables, and let us eat, and let us drink water: 
\v 13 and let our appearance be seen by you, and the appearance of the children who eat \add at\add* the king's table; and deal with your servants according as you will see. 
\p
\v 14 He listened to them, and proved them ten days. 
\v 15 At the end of the ten days their appearance was fairer and stronger in flesh than the children that fed at the king's table. 
\v 16 So Amelsad took away their supper and the wine of their drink, and gave them \fqa Gr. \ft seeds.\f*vegetables. 
\p
\v 17 And \add as for\add* these four children, God gave them understanding and prudence in all learning and wisdom: and Daniel had understanding in all visions and dreams. 
\v 18 At the end of the days, \add after\add* which the king had given orders to bring them in, then the chief of the eunuchs brought them in before Nebuchadnezzar. 
\v 19 The king spoke with them; and there was not found out of them all any like Daniel, and Ananias, and Mishael, and Azariah: and they stood before the king. 
\v 20 In every matter of wisdom and knowledge wherein the king questioned them, he found them ten times wiser than all the enchanters and sorcerers that were in all his kingdom. 
\v 21 Daniel continued till the first year of king Cyrus. 
\c 2 
\p
\v 1 In the second year of \add his\add* reign Nebuchadnezzar dreamed a dream, and his spirit was troubled, and his sleep departed from him. 
\v 2 The king gave orders to call the enchanters, and the magicians, and the sorcerers, and the Chaldeans, to declare to the king his dreams. And they came and stood before the king. 
\p
\v 3 The king said to them, I have dreamed, and my spirit was troubled to know the dream. 
\v 4 The Chaldeans spoke to the king in the Syrian language, \add saying\add*, O king, live forever: tell the dream to your servants, and we will declare the interpretation. 
\v 5 The king answered the Chaldeans, The matter has gone from me: if you do not make known to me the dream and the interpretation, you will be destroyed, and your houses will be plundered. 
\v 6 But if you make known to me the dream and its interpretation, you will receive from me gifts and presents and much honour: only tell me the dream and its interpretation. 
\v 7 They answered a second time, and said, Let the king tell the dream to his servants, and we will declare the interpretation. 
\p
\v 8 The king answered and said, I truly know that you are trying to gain time, because you see that the matter has gone from me. 
\v 9 If then you do not tell me the dream, I know that you have concerted to utter before me a false and corrupt tale, until the time has past: tell me my dream, and I will know that you will also declare to me the interpretation of it. 
\v 10 The Chaldeans answered before the king, and said, There is no man upon the earth, who can make known the king's matter: because no great king or ruler asks such a question of an enchanter, magician, or Chaldean. 
\v 11 For the question which the king asks is difficult, and there is no one else who will answer it before the king, but the gods, whose dwelling isn't with any flesh. 
\p
\v 12 Then the king in rage and anger commanded to destroy all the wise men of Babylon. 
\v 13 So the decree went out, and they began to kill the wise men; and they sought Daniel and his fellows to kill \add them\add*. 
\p
\v 14 Then Daniel answered \add with\add* counsel and prudence to Arioch the captain of the royal guard, who had gone out to kill the wise men of Babylon; \add saying\add*, 
\v 15 Chief magistrate of the king, why has the peremptory command proceeded from the king? So Arioch made known the matter to Daniel. 
\v 16 Daniel asked the king to give him time, and that he might \add thus\add* declare to the king the interpretation of it. 
\v 17 So Daniel went into his house, and made known the matter to Ananias, and Mishael, and Azariah, his friends. 
\v 18 They sought mercies from the God of heaven concerning this mystery; that Daniel and his friends might not perish with the rest of the wise men of Babylon. 
\p
\v 19 Then the mystery was revealed to Daniel in a vision of the night: and Daniel blessed the God of heaven, and said, 
\p
\v 20 May the name of God be blessed from everlasting and to everlasting: for wisdom and understanding are his. 
\v 21 He changes times and seasons: he appoints kings, and removes \add them\add*, giving wisdom to the wise, and prudence to those who have understanding: 
\v 22 he reveals deep and secret \add matters\add*; knowing what is in darkness, and the light is with him. 
\v 23 I give thanks to you, and praise \add you\add*, O God of my fathers, for you have given me wisdom and power, and have made known to me the things which we asked of you; and you have made known to me the king's vision. 
\p
\v 24 Daniel came to Arioch, whom the king had appointed to destroy the wise men of Babylon, and said to him; Don't destroy the wise men of Babylon, but bring me in before the king, and I will declare the interpretation to the king. 
\v 25 Then Arioch in haste brought in Daniel before the king, and said to him, I have found a man of the children of the captivity of Judea, who will declare the interpretation to the king. 
\v 26 The king answered and said to Daniel, whose name was Belteshazzar, Can you declare to me the dream which I saw, and the interpretation of it? 
\p
\v 27 Daniel answered before the king, and said, The mystery which the king asks \add the explanation of\add* isn't \add in the power\add* of the wise men, magicians, enchanters, \add or\add* soothsayers to declare to the king. 
\v 28 But there is a God in heaven revealing mysteries, and he has made known to king Nebuchadnezzar what things must come to pass in the last days. Your dream, and the visions of your head upon your bed, are as follows, 
\v 29 O king: your thoughts upon your bed arose \add as to\add* what must come to pass hereafter: and he who reveals mysteries has made known to you what must come to pass. 
\v 30 Moreover, this mystery has not been revealed to me because of wisdom which is in me beyond all \add others\add* living, but for the sake of making known the interpretation to the king, that you might know the thoughts of your heart. 
\p
\v 31 You, O king, saw, and behold an image: that image was great, and the appearance of it excellent, standing before your face; and the form of it was terrible. 
\v 32 \add It was\add* an image, the head of which was of fine gold, its hands and breast and arms of silver, \add its\add* belly and thighs of brass, 
\v 33 its legs of iron, its feet, part of iron and part of earthenware. 
\v 34 You saw until a stone was cut out of a mountain without hands, and it struck the image upon its feet of iron and earthenware, and completely reduced them to powder. 
\v 35 Then once for all the earthenware, the iron, the brass, the silver, the gold, were ground to powder, and became as chaff from the summer threshingfloor; and the violence of the wind carried them away, and no place was found for them: and the stone which had struck the image became a great mountain, and filled all the earth. 
\v 36 This is the dream; and we will tell its interpretation before the king. 
\p
\v 37 You, O king, are a king of kings, to whom the God of heaven has given a powerful and strong and honourable kingdom, 
\v 38 in every place where the children of men dwell: and he has given into your hand the wild beasts of the field, and the birds of the sky and the fish of the sea, and he has made you lord of all. 
\v 39 You are the head of gold. And after you will arise another kingdom inferior to you, and a third kingdom which is the brass, which will have dominion over all the earth; 
\v 40 and a fourth kingdom, which will be strong as iron: as iron beats to powder and subdues all things, so will it beat to powder and subdue. 
\v 41 Because you saw the feet and the toes, part of earthenware and part of iron, the kingdom will be divided; yet there will be in it some of the strength of iron, as you saw the iron mixed with earthenware. 
\v 42 And \add because\add* the toes of the feet were part of iron and part of earthenware, part of the kingdom will be strong, and \add part\add* of it will be broken. 
\v 43 Because you saw the iron mixed with earthenware, they will be mingled with the seed of men: but they will not cling together, as the iron does not mix itself with earthenware. 
\p
\v 44 In the days of those kings the God of heaven will set up a kingdom which will never be destroyed: and his kingdom will not be left to another people, \add but\add* it will beat to pieces and grind to powder all \add other\add* kingdoms, and it will stand forever. 
\v 45 Because you saw that a stone was cut out of a mountain without hands, and it beat to pieces the earthenware, the iron, the brass, the silver, the gold; the great God has made known to the king what must happen hereafter: and the dream is true, and the interpretation of it sure. 
\p
\v 46 Then king Nebuchadnezzar fell upon his face, and worshipped Daniel, and gave orders to offer to him gifts and incense. 
\v 47 The king answered and said to Daniel, Of a truth your God is a God of gods, and Lord of kings, who reveals mysteries; for you have been able to reveal this mystery. 
\v 48 The king promoted Daniel, and gave him great and abundant gifts, and set him over the whole province of Babylon, and \add made him\add* chief satrap over all the wise men of Babylon. 
\v 49 Daniel asked of the king, and he appointed Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego, over the affairs of the province of Babylon: but Daniel was in the king's palace. 
\c 3 
\p
\v 1 In \add his\add* eighteenth year Nebuchadnezzar the king made a golden image, its height was sixty cubits, its breadth six cubits: and he set it up in the plain of Deira, in the province of Babylon. 
\v 2 He sent to gather the governors, and the captains, and the heads of provinces, chiefs, and princes, and those who were in authority, and all the rulers of districts, to come to the dedication of the image. 
\v 3 So the heads of provinces, the governors, the captains, the chiefs, the great princes, those who were in authority, and all the rulers of districts, were gathered to the dedication of the image which king Nebuchadnezzar had set up; and they stood before the image. 
\p
\v 4 Then a herald cried aloud, To you it is commanded, you peoples, tribes, \add and\add* languages, 
\v 5 whenever you hear the sound of the trumpet, and pipe, and harp, and sackbut, and psaltery, and every kind of music, you will fall down and worship the golden image which king Nebuchadnezzar has set up. 
\v 6 Whoever will not fall down and worship, in the same hour he will be cast into the burning fiery furnace. 
\v 7 It happened when the nations heard the sound of the trumpet, and pipe, and harp, and sackbut, and psaltery, and all kinds of music, all the nations, tribes, \add and\add* languages, fell down and worshipped the golden image which king Nebuchadnezzar had set up. 
\p
\v 8 Then \add certain\add* Chaldeans came near, and accused the Jews to the king, \add saying\add*, 
\v 9 O king, live forever. 
\v 10 You, O king, have made a decree, that every man who hears the sound of the trumpet, and pipe, and harp, sackbut, and psaltery, and all kinds of music, 
\v 11 and will not fall down and worship the golden image, will be cast into the burning fiery furnace. 
\v 12 There are \add certain\add* Jews whom you have appointed over the affairs of the province of Babylon, Shadrach, Meshach, \add and\add* Abednego, who have not obeyed your decree, O king: they don't serve your gods, and don't worship the golden image which you have set up. 
\p
\v 13 Then Nebuchadnezzar in wrath and anger commanded to bring Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego: and they were brought before the king. 
\v 14 Nebuchadnezzar answered and said to them, Is it true, Shadrach, Meshach, \add and\add* Abednego, that you don't serve my gods, and don't worship the golden image which I have set up? 
\v 15 Now then if you are ready, whenever you hear the sound of the trumpet, and pipe, and harp, and sackbut, and psaltery, and harmony, and every kind of music, to fall down and worship the golden image which I have made; \add well\add*: but if you don't worship, in the same hour you will be cast into the burning fiery furnace; and who is the God who will deliver you out of my hand? 
\p
\v 16 Then Shadrach, Meshach, \add and\add* Abednego answered, and said to king Nebuchadnezzar, We have no need to answer you concerning this matter. 
\v 17 For our God whom we serve is in the heavens, able to deliver us from the burning fiery furnace, and he will rescue us from your hands, O king. 
\v 18 But if not, be it known to you, O king, that we \add will\add* not serve your gods, nor worship the image which you have set up. 
\p
\v 19 Then Nebuchadnezzar was filled with wrath, and the form of his face was changed toward Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego: and he gave orders to heat the furnace seven times \add more than usual\add*, until it should burn to the uttermost. 
\v 20 He commanded mighty men to bind Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego, and to cast \add them\add* into the burning fiery furnace. 
\v 21 Then those men were bound with their trousers, turbans, leggings, and other garments, and were cast into the midst of the burning fiery furnace, 
\v 22 because the king's word prevailed; and the furnace was made exceeding hot. 
\v 23 Then these three men, Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego, fell bound into the midst of the burning furnace, 
\v 24 and walked among the flame, singing praise to God, and blessing the Lord. 
\p
\v 25 Then Azariah stood up, and prayed on this manner; and opening his mouth among the fire said, 
\p
\v 26 You are blessed, O Lord God of our fathers: your name is worthy to be praised and glorified for evermore: 
\v 27 for you are righteous in all the things that you have done to us: yes, true are all your works, your ways are right, and all your judgments truth. 
\p
\v 28 In all the things that you have brought upon us, and upon the holy city of our fathers, \add even\add* Jerusalem, you have executed true judgment: for according to truth and judgment you brought all these things upon us because of our sins. 
\v 29 For we have sinned and committed wickedness, departing from you. In all things have we trespassed, and not obeyed your commandments, 
\v 30 nor kept them, neither done as you have commanded us, that it might go well with us. 
\v 31 So all that you have brought upon us, and every thing that you have done to us, you have done in true judgment. 
\p
\v 32 You delivered us into the hands of lawless enemies, most hateful forsakers \add of God\add*, and to an unjust king, and the most wicked in all the world. 
\v 33 Now we cannot open our mouths, we have become a shame and reproach to your servants, and to those who worship you. 
\p
\v 34 But don't wholly deliver us up, for your name's sake, neither cancel your covenant: 
\v 35 and don't cause your mercy to depart from us, for your beloved Abraham's sake, for your servant Isaac's sake, and for your holy Israel's sake; 
\v 36 to whom you have spoken and promised, that you would multiply their seed as the stars of heaven, and as the sand that lies upon the seashore. 
\v 37 For, Master, we have been diminished more than any nation, and are humbled this day in all the world because of our sins. 
\v 38 Neither is there at this time prince, or prophet, or leader, or burnt offering, or sacrifice, or oblation, or incense, or place to sacrifice before you, and to find mercy. 
\p
\v 39 Nevertheless in a contrite heart and a humble spirit let us be accepted. As with the burnt offerings of rams and bullocks, and as with ten thousands of fat lambs: 
\v 40 so let our sacrifice be in your sight this day, and \add grant\add* that we may wholly go after you: for they that put their trust in you will not be greatly agitated.
\p
\v 41 Now we follow you with all our heart, we fear you, and seek your face. Don't put us to shame: 
\v 42 but deal with us after your lovingkindness, and according to the multitude of your mercies. 
\p
\v 43 Deliver us also according to your marvellous works, and give glory to your name, O Lord: 
\v 44 and let all those who do your servants harm be ashamed; and let them be greatly agitated in all their power and might, and let their strength be broken; 
\v 45 and let them know that you are Lord, the only God, and glorious over the whole world. 
\p
\v 46 The king's servants who put them in, didn't stop making the oven hot with rosin, pitch, tow, and small wood; 
\v 47 so that the flame streamed forth above the furnace forty-nine cubits. 
\v 48 It passed through, and burned those Chaldeans it found about the furnace. 
\p
\v 49 But the angel of the Lord came down into the oven together with Azariah and his fellows, and struck the flame of the fire out of the oven; 
\v 50 and made the midst of the furnace as it had been a moist whistling wind, so that the fire touched them not at all, neither hurt nor troubled them. 
\p
\v 51 Then the three, as out of one mouth, praised, glorified, and blessed, God in the furnace, saying, 
\q1
\v 52 You are blessed, O Lord God of our fathers:
\q2 and to be praised and exalted above all forever.
\q1 And blessed is your glorious and holy name:
\q2 and to be praised and exalted above all forever. 
\q1
\v 53 You are blessed in the temple of your holy glory:
\q2 and to be praised and glorified above all forever.
\q1
\v 54 You are blessed who sees the depths, and sits upon the cherubim:
\q2 and to be praised and exalted above all forever. 
\q1
\v 55 You are blessed on the glorious throne of your kingdom: 
\q2 and to be praised and glorified above all forever. 
\q1
\v 56 You are blessed in the firmament of heaven:
\q2 and above all to be praised and glorified forever. 
\q1
\v 57 O all you works of the Lord, bless the Lord:
\q2 praise and exalt him above all forever. 
\q1
\v 58 O you heavens, bless the Lord:
\q2 praise and exalt him above all forever. 
\q1
\v 59 O you angels of the Lord, bless the Lord:
\q2 praise and exalt him above all forever. 
\q1
\v 60 O all you waters that be above the heaven, bless the Lord:
\q2 praise and exalt him above all forever. 
\q1
\v 61 O all you powers of the Lord, bless the Lord:
\q2 praise and exalt him above all forever. 
\q1
\v 62 O you sun and moon, bless the Lord:
\q2 praise and exalt him above all forever. 
\q1
\v 63 O you stars of heaven, bless the Lord:
\q2 praise and exalt him above all forever. 
\q1
\v 64 O every shower and dew, bless the Lord:
\q2 praise and exalt him above all forever. 
\q1
\v 65 O all you winds, bless the Lord:
\q2 praise and exalt him above all forever. 
\q1
\v 66 O you fire and heat, bless the Lord:
\q2 praise and exalt him above all forever. 
\q1
\v 71 O you nights and days, bless the Lord:
\q2 praise and exalt him above all forever. 
\q1
\v 72 O you light and darkness, bless the Lord:
\q2 praise and exalt him above all forever. 
\q1
\v 72a O you frost and heat, bless the Lord:
\q2 praise and exalt him above all forever. 
\q1
\v 72b O you frost and snow, bless the Lord:
\q2 praise and exalt him above all forever. 
\q1
\v 73 O you lightnings and clouds, bless the Lord:
\q2 praise and exalt him above all forever. 
\q1
\v 74 O let the earth bless the Lord:
\q2 praise and exalt him above all forever. 
\q1
\v 75 O you mountains and little hills, bless the Lord:
\q2 praise and exalt him above all forever. 
\q1
\v 76 O all you things that grow on the earth, bless the Lord:
\q2 praise and exalt him above all forever. 
\q1
\v 77 O you seas and rivers, bless the Lord:
\q2 praise and exalt him above all forever. 
\q1
\v 78 O you fountains, bless the Lord:
\q2 praise and exalt him above all forever. 
\q1
\v 79 O you whales, and all that move in the waters, bless the Lord:
\q2 praise and exalt him above all forever. 
\q1
\v 80 O all you fowls of the air, bless the Lord:
\q2 praise and exalt him above all forever. 
\q1
\v 81 O all you beasts and cattle, bless the Lord:
\q2 praise and exalt him above all forever. 
\q1
\v 82 O you children of men, bless the Lord:
\q2 praise and exalt him above all forever. 
\q1
\v 83 O Israel, bless the Lord:
\q2 praise and exalt him above all forever. 
\q1
\v 84 O you priests of the Lord, bless the Lord:
\q2 praise and exalt him above all forever. 
\q1
\v 85 O you servants of the Lord, bless the Lord:
\q2 praise and exalt him above all forever. 
\q1
\v 86 O you spirits and souls of the righteous, bless the Lord:
\q2 praise and exalt him above all forever. 
\q1
\v 87 O you holy and humble men of heart, bless the Lord:
\q2 praise and exalt him above all forever. 
\q1
\v 88 O Ananias, Azariah, and Mishael, bless the Lord;
\q2 praise and exalt him above all forever:
\q1 for he has delivered us from hell,
\q2 and saved us from the hand of death,
\q2 and delivered us out of the midst of the furnace \add and\add* burning flame:
\q2 even out of the midst of the fire he has delivered us. 
\q1
\v 89 O give thanks to the Lord, because he is gracious:
\q2 for his mercy \add endureth\add* forever. 
\q1
\v 90 O all you who worship the Lord, bless the God of gods, praise him and give him thanks:
\q2 for his mercy \add endureth\add* forever. 
\p
\v 91 Nebuchadnezzar heard them singing praises; and he wondered, and rose up in haste, and said to his nobles, Didn't we cast three men bound into the midst of the fire? And they said to the king, Yes, O king. 
\v 92 The king said, But I see four men loose, and walking among the fire, and no harm has happened to them; and the appearance of the fourth is like the Son of God. 
\v 93 Then Nebuchadnezzar drew near to the door of the burning fiery furnace, and said, Shadrach, Meshach, \add and\add* Abednego, you servants of the most high God, proceed forth, and come here. So Shadrach, Meshach, \add and\add* Abednego, came forth out of the midst of the fire. 
\v 94 Then were assembled the satraps, and captains, and heads of provinces, and the royal princes; and they saw the men, \add and perceived\add* that the fire had not had power against their bodies, and the hair of their head was not burnt, and their coats were not scorched, nor was the smell of fire upon them. 
\p
\v 95 King Nebuchadnezzar answered and said, Blessed be the God of Shadrach, Meshach, \add and\add* Abednego, who has sent his angel, and delivered his servants, because they trusted in him; and they have changed the king's word, and delivered their bodies to be burnt, that they might not serve nor worship any god, except their own God. 
\v 96 Therefore I publish a decree: Every people, tribe, \add or\add* language, that will speak reproachfully against the God of Shadrach, Meshach, \add and\add* Abednego, will be destroyed, and their houses will be plundered: because there is no other God who can deliver thus. 
\v 97 Then the king promoted Shadrach, Meshach, \add and\add* Abednego, in the province of Babylon, and advanced them, and gave them authority to rule over all the Jews who were in his kingdom. 
\c 4 
\p
\v 1 King Nebuchadnezzar to all nations, tribes, and tongues, who dwell in all the earth; Peace be multiplied to you. 
\v 2 It seemed good to me to declare to you the signs and wonders which the most high God has worked with me, 
\v 3 how great and mighty \add they are\add*: his kingdom is an everlasting kingdom, and his power to all generations. 
\p
\v 4 I Nebuchadnezzar was thriving in my house, and prospering. 
\v 5 I saw a vision, and it terrified me, and I was troubled on my bed, and the visions of my head troubled me. 
\v 6 I made a decree to bring in before me all the wise men of Babylon, that they might make known to me the interpretation of the dream. 
\v 7 So the enchanters, magicians, soothsayers, \add and\add* Chaldeans came in: and I told the dream before them; but they did not make known to me the interpretation of it; 
\v 8 until Daniel came, whose name is Belteshazzar, according to the name of my God, who has within him the Holy Spirit of God; to whom I said, 
\p
\v 9 O Belteshazzar, chief of the enchanters, of whom I know that the Holy Spirit of God is in you, and no mystery is too hard for you, hear the vision of my dream which I had, and tell me the interpretation of it. 
\v 10 I had a vision upon my bed; and behold a tree among the earth, and its height was great. 
\v 11 The tree grew large and strong, and its height reached to the sky, and its extent to the extremity of the whole earth: 
\v 12 its leaves were fair, and its fruit abundant, and in it was food for all; and under it the wild beasts of the field took shelter, and the birds of the sky lodged in its branches, and all flesh was fed from it. 
\p
\v 13 I beheld in the night vision upon my bed, and, behold, a watcher and a holy one came down from heaven, and cried aloud, and thus he said, 
\v 14 Cut down the tree, and pluck off its branches, and shake off its leaves, and scatter its fruit: let the wild beasts be removed from under it, and the birds from its branches. 
\v 15 Only leave the stump of its roots in the earth, and \add bind it\add* with an iron and brass band in the grass outside; and it will lie and in the dew of heaven, and its portion \add will be\add* with the wild beasts in the grass of the field. 
\v 16 His heart will be changed from that of man, and the heart of a wild beast will be given to him; and seven times will pass over him. 
\v 17 The matter is by the decree of \f + \fr 4:17 \fqa Gr. \ft ιρ is a transliteration of \fqa Aramaic \ft עִיר, watcher. Brenton translates this ``the watcher'' but the LXX text transliterates this as if it were a proper name so that is what has been done here.\f*Ir, and the demand is a word of the holy ones; that the living may know that the Most High is Lord \add over\add* the kingdom of men, and he will give it to whoever he pleases, and will set up over it the lowliest of men. 
\v 18 This is the vision which I king Nebuchadnezzar saw: now you, Belteshazzar, declare the interpretation, for none of the wise men of my kingdom are able to show me the interpretation of it: but you, Daniel, are able; for the Holy Spirit of God is in you. 
\p
\v 19 Then Daniel, whose name is Belteshazzar, was stunned about one hour, and his thoughts troubled him. The king answered and said, Belteshazzar, do not let the dream or its interpretation trouble you. And Belteshazzar answered and said, \add My\add* lord, let the dream be to those who hate you, and its interpretation to your enemies. 
\v 20 The tree which you saw, that grew large and strong, whose height reached to the sky and its extent to all the earth; 
\v 21 and whose leaves were flourishing, and its fruit abundant, and it was food for all; under it the wild beasts lodged, and the birds of the sky took shelter in its branches: 
\v 22 is yourself, O king; for you have grown great and powerful, and your greatness has increased and reached to heaven, and your dominion to the ends of the earth. 
\v 23 Because the king saw \f + \fr 4:23 \ft See the note to 4:17.\f*Ir and a holy one coming down from heaven, and he said, Strip the tree, and destroy it; only leave the stump of its roots in the ground, and \add bind it\add* with a band of iron and brass in the grass outside; and it will lie in the dew of heaven, and its portion will be with wild beasts, until seven times have passed over it; 
\v 24 this is the interpretation of it, O king, and it is a decree of the Most High, which has come upon my lord the king. 
\v 25 They will drive you forth from men, and your dwelling will be with wild beasts, and they will feed you with grass as an ox, and you will have your lodging under the dew of heaven, and seven times will pass over you, until you know that the Most High is Lord of the kingdom of men, and will give it to whom he pleases. 
\v 26 Because they said, Leave the stumps of the roots of the tree; your kingdom remains yours after you come to know the authority of heaven.
\v 27 Therefore, O king, let my counsel please you, and atone for your sins by alms, and \add your\add* iniquities by compassion on the poor: it may be God will be long-suffering to your trespasses. 
\p
\v 28 All these things came upon king Nebuchadnezzar. 
\v 29 After twelve months, as he walked in his palace in Babylon, 
\v 30 the king answered and said, Isn't this great Babylon, which I have built for a royal residence, by the might of my power, for the honour of my glory? 
\p
\v 31 While the word was yet in the king's mouth, there came a voice from heaven, \add saying\add*, To you, king Nebuchadnezzar, they say, The kingdom has departed from you. 
\v 32 They \add will\add* drive you from men, and your dwelling will be with the wild beasts of the field, and they will feed you with grass as an ox: and seven times will pass over you, until you know that the Most High is Lord of the kingdom of men, and he will give it to whoever he will please. 
\p
\v 33 In the same hour the word was fulfilled upon Nebuchadnezzar: and he was driven forth from men, and he ate grass as an ox, and his body was bathed with the dew of heaven, until his hairs were grown like lions' \add hairs\add*, and his nails as birds' \add claws\add*. 
\p
\v 34 At the end of the time I Nebuchadnezzar lifted up my eyes to heaven, and my reason returned to me, and I blessed the Most High, and praised him that lives forever, and gave \add him\add* glory; for his dominion is an everlasting dominion, and his kingdom \add lasts\add* to all generations: 
\v 35 and all the inhabitants of the earth are reputed as nothing: and he does according to his will in the army of heaven, and among the inhabitants of the earth: and there is no one who will withstand his power, and say to him, What have you done? 
\v 36 At the same time my reason returned to me, and I came to the honour of my kingdom; and my \add natural\add* form returned to me, and my princes, and my nobles, sought me, and I was established in my kingdom, and more abundant majesty was added to me. 
\p
\v 37 Now therefore I Nebuchadnezzar praise and greatly exalt and glorify the King of heaven; for all his works are true, and his paths are judgment: and all who walk in pride he is able to abase. 
\c 5 
\p
\v 1 \f + \fr 5:1 \ft In the Greek text, Belshazzar (the king) and Belteshazzar (Daniel) are both written as Βαλτασαρ 'Baltasar'.\f*Belshazzar the king made a great supper for his thousand nobles, and \add there was\add* wine before the thousand. 
\v 2 Belshazzar, while drinking wine, gave orders that they should bring the gold and silver vessels, which Nebuchadnezzar his father had brought forth from the temple in Jerusalem; that the king, and his nobles, and his mistresses, and his concubines, should drink out of them. 
\v 3 So the gold and silver vessels were brought which \add Nebuchadnezzar\add* had taken out of the temple of God in Jerusalem; and the king, and his nobles, and his mistresses, and his concubines, drank out of them. 
\v 4 They drank wine, and praised the gods of gold, and of silver, and of brass, and of iron, and of wood, and of stone. 
\p
\v 5 In the same hour fingers of a man's hand came forth, and wrote in front of the lamp on the plaster of the wall of the king's house: and the king saw the knuckles of the hand that wrote. 
\v 6 Then the king's face changed, and his thoughts troubled him, and the joints of his loins were loosed, and his knees struck one another. 
\v 7 The king cried aloud to bring in the magicians, Chaldeans, \add and\add* soothsayers; and he said to the wise men of Babylon, Whoever will read this writing, and make known to me the interpretation, will be clothed with scarlet, and \add there will be\add* a golden chain upon his neck, and he will be the third ruler in my kingdom. 
\v 8 Then came in all the king's wise men: but they could not read the writing, nor make known the interpretation to the king. 
\v 9 King Belshazzar was troubled, and his face changed upon him, and his nobles were troubled with him. 
\p
\v 10 Then the queen came into the banquet house, and said, O king, live forever: don't let your thoughts trouble you, and don't let your face be changed. 
\v 11 There is a man in your kingdom, in whom is the Spirit of God; and in the days of your father watchfulness and understanding were found in him; and king Nebuchadnezzar your father made him chief of the enchanters, magicians, Chaldeans, \add and\add* soothsayers. 
\v 12 For \add there is\add* an excellent spirit in him, and sense and understanding are in him, interpreting dreams \add as he does\add*, and answering hard \add questions\add*, and solving difficulties: \add it is\add* Daniel, and the king gave him the name of Belteshazzar: now then let him be called, and he will tell you the interpretation of the writing. 
\p
\v 13 Then Daniel was brought in before the king: and the king said to Daniel, Are you Daniel, of the children of the captivity of Judea, which the king my father brought? 
\v 14 I have heard concerning you, that the Spirit of God is in you, and \add that\add* watchfulness and understanding and excellent wisdom have been found in you. 
\v 15 Now the wise men, magicians, \add and\add* soothsayers have come in before me, to read the writing, and make known to me the interpretation: but they could not tell it me. 
\v 16 I have heard concerning you, that you are able to make interpretations: now then if you will be able to read the writing, and to make known to me the interpretation of it, you will be clothed with purple, and there will be a golden chain upon your neck, and you will be third ruler in my kingdom. 
\p
\v 17 Daniel said before the king, Let your gifts be to yourself, and give the present of your house to another; but I will read the writing, and will make known to you the interpretation of it. 
\v 18 O king, the most high God gave to your father Nebuchadnezzar a kingdom, and majesty, and honour, and glory: 
\v 19 and because of the majesty which he gave to him, all nations, tribes \add and\add* languages trembled and feared before him: whoever he wanted he killed; and whoever he wanted he struck; and whoever he wanted he exalted; and whoever he wanted he abased. 
\v 20 But when his heart was lifted up, and his spirit was emboldened to act proudly, he was deposed from his royal throne, and \add his\add* honour was taken from him. 
\v 21 He was driven forth from men; and his heart was given him after the nature of wild beasts, and his dwelling was with the wild donkeys; and they fed him with grass as an ox, and his body was bathed with the dew of heaven; until he knew that the most high God is Lord of the kingdom of men, and will give it to whoever he will please. 
\p
\v 22 You accordingly, his son, O Belshazzar, have not humbled your heart before God: don't you know all this? 
\v 23 You have been exalted against the Lord God of heaven; and they have brought before you the vessels of his house, and you, and your nobles, and your mistresses, and your concubines, have drunk wine out of them; and you have praised the gods of gold, and silver, and brass, and iron, and wood, and stone, which don't see, and which don't hear, and don't know: and the God in whose hand are your breath and all your ways have you not glorified. 
\v 24 Therefore from his presence has been sent the knuckle of a hand; and he has ordered this writing. 
\p
\v 25 This is the ordered writing, Mane, Thekel, Phares. 
\v 26 This is the interpretation of the sentence: Mane; God has measured your kingdom, and finished it. 
\v 27 Thekel; it has been weighed in the balance, and found wanting. 
\v 28 Phares; your kingdom is divided, and given to the Medes and Persians. 
\p
\v 29 Then Belteshazzar commanded, and they clothed Daniel with scarlet, and put the golden chain about his neck, and proclaimed concerning him that he was the third ruler in the kingdom. 
\v 30 In the same night Belteshazzar the Chaldean king was killed. 
\c 6 
\nb
\v 1 Darius the Mede succeeded to the kingdom, being sixty-two years \add old\add*. 
\p
\v 2 It pleased Darius, and he set over the kingdom a hundred and twenty satraps, to be in all his kingdom; 
\v 3 and over them three governors, of whom one was Daniel; for the satraps to give account to them, that the king should not be troubled. 
\v 4 Daniel was over them, for \add there was\add* an excellent spirit in him; and the king set him over all his kingdom. 
\p
\v 5 Then the governors and satraps sought to find occasion against Daniel; but they found against him no occasion, nor trespass, nor error, because he was faithful. 
\v 6 The governors said, We will not find occasion against Daniel, except in the ordinances of his God. 
\p
\v 7 Then the governors and satraps stood by the king, and said to him, King Darius, live forever. 
\v 8 All who preside over your kingdom, captains and satraps, chiefs and local governors, have taken counsel together, to establish by a royal statute and to confirm a decree, that whoever will ask a petition of any god or man for thirty days, save of you, O king, will be cast into the den of lions. 
\v 9 Now then, O king, establish the decree, and publish a writ, that the decree of the Persians and Medes don't be changed. 
\v 10 Then king Darius commanded the decree to be written. 
\p
\v 11 When Daniel knew that the decree was ordered, he went into his house; and his windows were opened in his chambers toward Jerusalem, and three times in the day he knelt upon his knees, and prayed and gave thanks before his God, as he used to do before. 
\p
\v 12 Then these men watched, and found Daniel praying and supplicating to his God. 
\v 13 They came and said to the king, O king, have you not made a decree, that whatever man will ask a petition of any god or man for thirty days, but of you, O king, will be cast into the den of lions? And the king said, The word is true, and the decree of the Medes and Persians will not pass. 
\v 14 Then they answered and said before the king, Daniel of the children of the captivity of Judea, has not submitted to your decree; and three times in the day he makes his requests of his God. 
\v 15 Then the king, when he heard the saying, was much grieved for Daniel, and he greatly exerted himself for Daniel to deliver him: and he exerted himself till evening to deliver him. 
\p
\v 16 Then those men said to the king, Know, O king, that the law of the Medes and Persians is, that we must not change any decree or statute which the king will make. 
\v 17 Then the king commanded, and they brought Daniel, and cast him into the den of lions. But the king said to Daniel, Your God whom you serve continually, he will deliver you. 
\v 18 They brought a stone, and put it on the mouth of the den; and the king sealed \add it\add* with his ring, and with the ring of his nobles; that the case might not be altered with regard to Daniel. 
\v 19 The king departed to his house, and lay down fasting, and they brought him no food; and his sleep departed from him. But God shut the mouths of the lions, and they did not molest Daniel. 
\p
\v 20 Then the king arose very early in the morning, and came in haste to the den of lions. 
\v 21 When he drew near to the den, he cried with a loud voice, Daniel, servant of the living God, has your God, whom you serve continually, been able to deliver you from the lion's mouth? 
\v 22 Daniel said to the king, O king, live forever. 
\v 23 My God has sent his angel, and stopped the lions' mouths, and they have not hurt me: for uprightness was found in me before him; and moreover before you, O king, I have committed no trespass. 
\v 24 Then the king was very glad for him, and he commanded to bring Daniel out of the den. So Daniel was brought out of the den, and there was found no hurt upon him, because he believed in his God. 
\p
\v 25 The king commanded, and they brought the men who had accused Daniel, and they were cast into the den of lions, they, and their children, and their wives: and they reached not the bottom of the den before the lions had the mastery of them, and completely broke to pieces all their bones. 
\p
\v 26 Then king Darius wrote to all nations, tribes, \add and\add* languages, who dwell in all the earth, \add saying\add*, Peace be multiplied to you. 
\v 27 This decree has been set forth by me in every dominion of my kingdom, that \add men\add* tremble and fear before the God of Daniel: for he is the living and eternal God, and his kingdom will not be destroyed, and his dominion is forever. 
\v 28 He helps and delivers, and works signs and wonders in the heaven and on the earth, who has rescued Daniel from the power of the lions. 
\v 29 Daniel prospered in the reign of Darius, and in the reign of Cyrus the Persian. 
\c 7 
\p
\v 1 In the first year of Belteshazzar king of the Chaldeans Daniel had a dream, and visions of his head upon his bed: and he wrote his dream. 
\p
\v 2 I Daniel beheld, and, lo, the four winds of heaven blew violently upon the great sea. 
\v 3 There came up four great beasts out of the sea, differing from one another. 
\v 4 The first \add was\add* as a lioness, and her wings as an eagle's: I beheld until her wings were plucked, and she was lifted off from the earth, and she stood on human feet, and a man's heart was given to her. 
\v 5 And, behold, a second beast like a bear, and it supported itself on one side, and there were three ribs in its mouth, between its teeth: and thus they said to it, Arise, devour much flesh. 
\v 6 After this one I looked, and behold another wild beast as a leopard, and it had four wings of a bird upon it: and the wild beast had four heads, and power was given to it. 
\v 7 After this one I looked, and behold a fourth beast, dreadful and terrible, and exceedingly strong, and its teeth were of iron; devouring and crushing to atoms, and it trampled the remainder with its feet: and it was altogether different from all the beasts that were before it; and it \add had\add* ten horns. 
\v 8 I noticed his horns, and, behold, another little horn came up among them, and before it three of the former horns were rooted out: and, behold, \add there were\add* eyes as the eyes of a man in this horn, and a mouth speaking great things. 
\p
\v 9 I beheld until the thrones were set, and the Ancient of days sat; and his clothing was white as snow, and the hair of his head as pure wool: his throne was a flame of fire, \add and\add* his wheels burning fire. 
\v 10 A stream of fire rushed forth before him: thousand thousands ministered to him, and ten thousands of myriads attended upon him: the judgment sat, and the books were opened. 
\v 11 I beheld then because of the voice of the great words which that horn spoke, until the wild beast was killed and destroyed, and his body given to be burnt with fire. 
\v 12 The dominion of the rest of the wild beasts was taken away; but a prolonging of life was given them for certain times. 
\p
\v 13 I beheld in the night vision, and, lo, \add one\add* coming with the clouds of heaven as the Son of man, and he came on to the Ancient of days, and was brought near to him. 
\v 14 To him was given the dominion, and the honour, and the kingdom; and all nations, tribes, and languages, will serve him: his dominion is an everlasting dominion, which will not pass away, and his kingdom will not be destroyed. 
\p
\v 15 \add As for\add* me Daniel, my spirit in my body trembled, and the visions of my head troubled me. 
\v 16 I drew near to one of those who stood by, and I sought to learn of him the truth of all these things: and he told me the truth, and made known to me the interpretation of the things. 
\v 17 These four beasts are four kingdoms \add that\add* will rise up on the earth: 
\v 18 which will be taken away; and the saints of the Most High will take the kingdom, and possess it forever and ever. 
\p
\v 19 Then I enquired carefully concerning the fourth beast; for it differed from every \add other\add* beast, exceeding dreadful: its teeth were of iron, and its claws of brass, devouring, and completely breaking to pieces, and it trampled the remainder with its feet: 
\v 20 and concerning its ten horns that were in its head, and the other that came up, and rooted up \add some\add* of the former, which had eyes, and a mouth speaking great things, and his look was bolder than the rest. 
\v 21 I beheld, and that horn made war with the saints, and prevailed against them; 
\v 22 until the Ancient of days came, and he gave judgment to the saints of the Most High; and the time came on, and the saints possessed the kingdom. 
\v 23 He said, The fourth beast will be the fourth kingdom on the earth, which will excel all \add other\add* kingdoms, and will devour the whole earth, and trample and destroy it. 
\v 24 His ten horns are ten kings \add that\add* will arise: and after them will arise another, who will exceed all the former ones in wickedness, and he will subdue three kings. 
\v 25 He will speak words against the Most High, and will wear out the saints of the Most High, and will think to change times and law: and \add power\add* will be given into his hand for a time and times and half a time. 
\v 26 The judgment has sat, and they will remove \add his\add* dominion to abolish it, and to destroy it completely. 
\v 27 The kingdom and the power and the greatness of the kings that are under the whole heaven were given to the saints of the Most High; and his kingdom is an everlasting kingdom, and all powers will serve and obey him. 
\p
\v 28 Here is the end of the matter. As for me Daniel, my thoughts greatly troubled me, and my face was changed: but I kept the matter in my heart. 
\c 8 
\p
\v 1 In the third year of the reign of king Belteshazzar a vision appeared to me, \add even\add* to me Daniel, after that which appeared to me at the first. 
\v 2 I was in Susa the palace, which is in the land of Ælam, and I was on the \add bank of\add* Ubal. 
\v 3 I lifted up my eyes, and saw, and, behold, a ram standing in front of the Ubal; and he had high horns; and one was higher than the other, and the high one came up last. 
\v 4 I saw the ram butting westward, and northward, and southward; and no beast could stand before him, and there was none that could deliver out of his hand; and he did according to his will, and became great. 
\p
\v 5 I was considering, and, behold, a he-goat came from the south-west on the face of the whole earth, and touched not the earth: and the goat \add had\add* a horn between his eyes. 
\v 6 He came to the ram that had the horns, which I had seen standing in front of the Ubal, and he ran at him with the violence of his strength. 
\v 7 I saw him coming up close to the ram, and he was furiously enraged against him, and he struck the ram, and broke both his horns: and there was no strength in the ram to stand before him, but he cast him on the ground, and trampled on him; and there was none that could deliver the ram out of his hand. 
\p
\v 8 The he-goat grew exceedingly great: and when he was strong, his great horn was broken; and four other \add horns\add* rose up in its place toward the four winds of heaven. 
\v 9 Out of one of them came forth one strong horn, and it grew very great toward the south, and toward the host: 
\v 10 and it magnified itself to the host of heaven; and there fell to the earth \add some\add* of the host of heaven and of the stars, and they trampled on them. 
\v 11 And \add this will be\add* until the chief captain will have delivered the captivity: and because of him the sacrifice was disturbed, and he prospered; and the holy place will be made desolate. 
\v 12 A sin-offering was given for the sacrifice, and righteousness was cast down to the ground; and it practised, and prospered. 
\v 13 I heard one saint speaking, and a saint said to a certain one speaking, How long will the vision continue, \add even\add* the removal of the sacrifice, and the bringing in of the sin of desolation; and \add how long\add* will the sanctuary and host be trampled? 
\v 14 He said to him, Evening and morning \add there will be\add* two thousand and four hundred days; and \add then\add* the sanctuary will be cleansed. 
\p
\v 15 It happened, as I, \add even\add* I Daniel, saw the vision, and sought to understand it, that, behold, there stood before me as the appearance of a man. 
\v 16 I heard the voice of a man between \add the banks of\add* the Ubal; and he called, and said, Gabriel, cause that man to understand the vision. 
\v 17 He came and stood near where I stood: and when he came, I was struck with awe, and fell upon my face: but he said to me, Understand, son of man: for yet the vision is for an appointed time. 
\v 18 While he spoke with me, I fell upon my face to the earth: and he touched me, and set me on my feet. 
\v 19 He said, Behold, I make you know the things that will come to pass at the end of the wrath: for the vision \add is\add* yet for an appointed time. 
\p
\v 20 The ram which you saw that had the horns is the king of the Medes and Persians. 
\v 21 The he-goat is the king of the Greeks: and the great horn which was between his eyes, he is the first king. 
\v 22 And \add as for\add* the one that was broken, in whose place there stood up four horns, four kings will arise out of his nation, but not in their \add own\add* strength. 
\v 23 At the latter time of their kingdom, when their sins are coming to the full, there will arise a king bold in face, and understanding riddles. 
\v 24 His power \add will be\add* great, and he will destroy wonderfully, and prosper, and practise, and will destroy mighty men, and the holy people. 
\v 25 The yoke of his chain will prosper: \add there is\add* craft in his hand; and he will magnify himself in his heart, and by craft will destroy many, and he will stand up for the destruction of many, and will crush them as eggs in his hand. 
\v 26 The vision of the evening and morning that was mentioned is true: seal the vision; for \add it is\add* for many days. 
\p
\v 27 I Daniel fell asleep, and was sick: then I arose, and did the king's business; and I wondered at the vision, and there was none that understood \add it\add*. 
\c 9 
\p
\v 1 In the first year of Darius the son of Assuerus, of the seed of the Medes, who reigned over the kingdom of the Chaldeans, 
\v 2 I Daniel understood by books the number of the years which was the word of the Lord to the prophet Jeremiah, \add even\add* seventy years for the accomplishment of the desolation of Jerusalem. 
\p
\v 3 I set my face toward the Lord God, to seek \add him\add* diligently by prayer and supplications, with fastings and sackcloth. 
\v 4 I prayed to the Lord my God, and confessed, and said, O Lord, the great and wonderful God, keeping your covenant and your mercy to those who love you, and to those who keep your commandments; we have sinned, 
\v 5 we have done wickedness, we have transgressed, and we have departed and turned aside from your commandments and from your judgments: 
\v 6 and we have not listened to your servants the prophets, who spoke in your name to our kings, and our princes, and our fathers, and to all the people of the land. 
\v 7 To you, O Lord, \add belongs\add* righteousness, and to us confusion of face, as at this day; to the men of Judah, and to the dwellers in Jerusalem, and to all Israel, to those who are near, and to those who are far off in all the earth, wherever you have scattered them, for the sin which they committed. 
\v 8 In you, O Lord, is our righteousness, and to us \add belongs\add* confusion of face, and to our kings, and to our princes, and to our fathers, because we have sinned. 
\v 9 To you, the Lord our God, \add belong\add* compassions and forgivenesses, because we have departed \add from you\add*; 
\v 10 neither have we listened to the voice of the Lord our God, to walk in his laws, which he set before us by the hands of his servants the prophets. 
\p
\v 11 Moreover all Israel have transgressed your law, and have refused to listen to your voice; so the curse has come upon us, and the oath that is written in the law of Moses the servant of God, because we have sinned against him. 
\v 12 He has confirmed his words, which he spoke against us, and against our judges who judged us, \add by\add* bringing upon us great evils, such as have not happened under the whole heaven, according to what has happened in Jerusalem. 
\v 13 As it is written in the law of Moses, all these evils have come upon us: yet we have not besought the Lord our God, that we might turn away from our iniquities, and have understanding in all your truth. 
\v 14 The Lord also has watched, and brought the evils upon us: for the Lord our God is righteous in all his work which he has executed, but we have not listened to his voice. 
\v 15 Now, O Lord our God, who brought your people out of the land of Egypt with a mighty hand, and made to yourself a name, as \add at\add* this day; we have sinned, we have transgressed. 
\p
\v 16 O Lord, your mercy is over all: let, I pray you, your wrath turn away, and your anger from your city Jerusalem, \add even\add* your holy mountain: for we have sinned, and because of our iniquities, and those of our fathers, Jerusalem and your people have become a reproach among all that are all around us. 
\v 17 Now, O Lord our God, listen to the prayer of your servant, and his supplications, and cause your face to shine on your desolate sanctuary, for your \add own\add* sake, O Lord. 
\v 18 Incline your ear, O my God, and hear; open your eyes and behold our desolation, and that of your city on which your name is called: for we do not bring our pitiful case before you on \add the ground of\add* our righteousness, but on \add the ground of\add* your manifold compassions, O Lord. 
\v 19 Listen, O Lord; be propitious, O Lord; pay attention, O Lord; delay not, O my God, for your own sake: for your name is called upon your city and upon your people. 
\p
\v 20 While I was still speaking, and praying, and confessing my sins and the sins of my people Israel, and bringing my pitiful case before the Lord my God concerning the holy mountain; 
\v 21 yes, while I was still speaking in prayer, behold the man Gabriel, whom I had seen in the vision at the beginning, \add came\add* flying, and he touched me about the hour of the evening sacrifice. 
\v 22 He instructed me, and spoke with me, and said, O Daniel, I have now come forth to impart to you understanding. 
\v 23 At the beginning of your supplication the word came forth, and I have come to tell you; for you are a man much beloved: therefore consider the matter, understand the vision. 
\p
\v 24 Seventy weeks have been determined upon your people, and upon the holy city, for sin to be ended, and to seal up transgressions, and to blot out the iniquities, and to make atonement for iniquities, and to bring in everlasting righteousness, and to seal the vision and the prophet, and to anoint the Most Holy. 
\p
\v 25 You will know and understand, that from the going forth of the command for the answer and for the building of Jerusalem until Christ the prince \add there will be\add* seven weeks, and sixty-two weeks: and then \add the time\add* will return, and the street will be built, and the wall, and the times will be exhausted. 
\p
\v 26 After the sixty-two weeks, the anointed one will be destroyed, and there is no judgment in him: and he will destroy the city and the sanctuary with the prince that is coming: they will be cut off with a flood, and to the end of the war which is rapidly completed he will appoint \add the city\add* to desolations. 
\p
\v 27 One week will establish the covenant with many: and among the week my sacrifice and drink-offering will be taken away: and on the temple \add will be\add* the abomination of desolations; and at the end of the time an end will be put to the desolation. 
\c 10 
\p
\v 1 In the third year of Cyrus king of the Persians a thing was revealed to Daniel, whose name was called Belteshazzar; and the thing was true, and great power and understanding in the vision was given to him. 
\v 2 In those days I Daniel was mourning three full weeks. 
\v 3 I ate no pleasant bread, and no flesh or wine entered into my mouth, neither did I anoint myself with oil, until three whole weeks were accomplished. 
\p
\v 4 On the twenty-fourth day of the first month, I was near the great river, which is Tigris Eddekel. 
\v 5 I lifted up my eyes, and looked, and behold a man clothed in linen, and his loins were girt with gold of Ophaz: 
\v 6 and his body was as Tharsis, and his face was as the appearance of lightning, and his eyes as lamps of fire, and his arms and his legs as the appearance of shining brass, and the voice of his words as the voice of a multitude. 
\v 7 Only I Daniel saw the vision: and the men who were with me didn't see the vision; but a great amazement fell upon them, and they fled in fear. 
\v 8 So I was left alone, and saw this great vision, and there was no strength left in me, and my glory was turned into corruption, and I retained no strength. 
\v 9 Yet I heard the voice of his words: and when I heard him I was pricked \add in the heart\add*, and \add I fell with\add* my face to the earth. 
\p
\v 10 And, behold, a hand touched me, and it raised me on my knees. 
\v 11 He said to me, O Daniel, man greatly beloved, understand the words which I speak to you, and stand upright: for I am now sent to you. And when he had spoken to me this word, I stood trembling. 
\v 12 He said to me, Don't be afraid, Daniel: for from the first day that you did set your heart to understand, and to afflict yourself before the Lord your God, your words were heard, and I have come because of your words. 
\v 13 But the prince of the kingdom of the Persians withstood me twenty-one days: and behold, Michael, one of the princes, came to help me; and I left him there with the chief of the kingdom of the Persians: 
\v 14 and I have come to inform you of all that will befall your people in the last days: for the vision is yet for \add many\add* days. 
\v 15 When he had spoken with me according to these words, I turned my face to the ground, and was pricked \add in the heart\add*. 
\p
\v 16 And, behold, as it were the likeness of a son of man touched my lips; and I opened my mouth, and spoke, and said to him that stood before me, O \add my\add* lord, at the sight of you my bowels were turned within me, and I had no strength. 
\v 17 How will your servant be able, O \add my\add* lord, to speak with this my lord? And as for me, from now on strength will not remain in me, and there is no breath left in me. 
\v 18 There touched me again as it were the appearance of a man, and he strengthened me, 
\v 19 and said to me, Don't be afraid, man greatly beloved: peace be to you, be courageous, and be strong. And when he had spoken with me, I received strength, and said, Let my lord speak; for you have strengthened me. 
\p
\v 20 He said, Do you know why I have come to you? And now I will return to fight with the prince of the Persians: and I was going in, and the prince of the Greeks came. 
\v 21 But I will tell you that which is ordained in the scripture of truth: and there is no one that holds with me in these matters but Michael your prince. 
\c 11 
\p
\v 1 In the first year of Cyrus I stood to strengthen and confirm \add him\add*. 
\p
\v 2 Now I will tell you the truth. Behold, there will yet rise up three kings in Persia: and the fourth will be very far richer than all: and after he is master of his wealth, he will rise up against all the kingdoms of the Greeks. 
\p
\v 3 There will rise up a mighty king, and he will be lord of a great empire, and will do according to his will. 
\p
\v 4 When his kingdom stands up, it will be broken, and will be divided to the four winds of heaven; but not to his posterity, nor according to his dominion which he ruled over: for his kingdom will be plucked up, and \add given\add* to others beside these. 
\p
\v 5 The king of the south will be strong; and one of their princes will prevail against him, and will obtain a great dominion. 
\v 6 After his years they will associate; and the daughter of the king of the south will come to the king of the north, to make agreements with him: but she will not retain power of arm; neither will his seed stand: and she will be delivered up, and those who brought her, and the maiden, and he who strengthened her in these times. 
\p
\v 7 \add But\add* out of the flower of her root there will arise \add one on\add* his place, and will come against the host, and will enter into the strongholds of the king of the north, and will fight against them, and prevail. 
\v 8 Yes, he will carry their gods with their molten \add images, and\add* all their precious vessels of silver and gold into Egypt with a body of captives; and he will last longer than the king of the north. 
\v 9 He will enter into the kingdom of the king of the south, and will return to his own land. 
\p
\v 10 His sons will gather a multitude among many: and one will certainly come, and overflow, and pass through, and he will rest, and collect his strength. 
\v 11 The king of the south will be greatly enraged, and will come forth, and will war with the king of the north: and he will raise a great multitude; but the multitude will be delivered into his hand. 
\v 12 He will take the multitude, and his heart will be exalted; and he will cast down many thousands; but he will not prevail. 
\v 13 For the king of the north will return, and bring a multitude greater than the former, and at the end of the times of years an invading army will come with a great force, and with much substance. 
\p
\v 14 In those times many will rise up against the king of the south; and the children of the spoilers of your people will exalt themselves to establish the vision; and they will fail. 
\v 15 The king of the north will come in, and cast up a mound, and take strong cities: and the arms of the king of the south will withstand, and his chosen ones will rise up, but there will be no strength to stand. 
\v 16 He who comes in against him will do according to his will, and there is no one to stand before him: and he will stand in the land of beauty, and it will be consumed by his hand. 
\p
\v 17 He will set his face to come in with the force of his whole kingdom, and will cause everything to prosper with him: and he will give him the daughter of women to corrupt her: but she will not continue, nor be on his side. 
\v 18 He will turn his face to the islands, and will take many, and cause princes to cease from their reproach: nevertheless his own reproach will return to him. 
\v 19 Then he will turn back his face to the strength of his own land: but he will become weak, and fall, and not be found. 
\p
\v 20 There will arise out of his root one that will cause a plant of the kingdom to pass over his place, earning kingly glory: and yet in those days he will be broken, yet not openly, nor in war. 
\p
\v 21 \add One\add* will stand on his place, \add who\add* has been despise, and they have not put upon him the honour of the kingdom: but he will come in prosperously, and obtain the kingdom by deceitful ways. 
\v 22 The arms of him that overflows will be washed away as with a flood from before him, and will be broken, and \add so will be\add* the head of the covenant. 
\v 23 Because of the leagues made with him he will work deceit: and he will come up, and overpower them with a small nation. 
\v 24 He will enter with prosperity, and \add that\add* into fertile districts; and he will do what his fathers and his fathers' fathers have not done; he will scatter among them plunder, and spoils, and wealth; and he will devise plans against Egypt, even for a time. 
\v 25 His strength and his heart will be stirred up against the king of the south with a great force; and the king of the south will engage in war with a great and very strong force; but \add his forces\add* will not stand, for they will devise plans against him: 
\v 26 and they will eat his provisions, and will crush him, and he will carry away armies as with a flood, and many will fall down killed. 
\p
\v 27 And \add as for\add* both the kings, their hearts \add are set\add* upon mischief, and they will speak lies at one table; but it will not prosper; for yet the end is for a \add fixed\add* time. 
\v 28 He will return to his land with much substance; and his heart \add will be\add* against the holy covenant; and he will perform \add great deeds\add*, and return to his own land. 
\p
\v 29 At the \add set\add* time he will return, and will come into the south, but the last \add expedition\add* will \add not\add* be as the first. 
\v 30 For the Citians issuing forth will come against him, and he will be brought low, and will return, and will be incensed against the holy covenant: and he will do \add thus\add*, and will return, and have intelligence with those who have forsaken the holy covenant. 
\p
\v 31 Seeds will spring up out of him, and they will profane the sanctuary of strength, and they will remove the perpetual \add sacrifice\add*, and make the abomination desolate. 
\v 32 The transgressors will bring about a covenant by deceitful ways: but a people knowing their God will prevail, and do \add valiantly\add*. 
\v 33 The intelligent of the people will understand much: yet they will fall by the sword, and by flame, and by captivity, and by spoil of \add many\add* days. 
\v 34 When they are weak they will be helped with a little help: but many will attach themselves to them with treachery. 
\p
\v 35 And \add some\add* of those who understand will fall, to try them as with fire, and to test \add them\add*, and so they may be manifested at the time of the end, for the matter \add is\add* yet for a \add set\add* time. 
\p
\v 36 He will do according to his will, and the king will exalt and magnify himself against every god, and will speak great swelling words, and will prosper until the indignation will be accomplished: for it is coming to an end. 
\v 37 He will not regard any gods of his fathers, nor the desire of women, neither he will regard any deity: for he will magnify himself above all. 
\v 38 He will honour the god of forces on his place: and a god whom his fathers didn't know he will honour with gold, and silver, and precious stones, and desirable things. 
\v 39 He will do \add thus\add* in the strong places of refuge with a foreign god, and will increase his glory: and he will subject many to them, and will distribute the land in gifts. 
\p
\v 40 At the end of the time he will conflict with the king of the south: and the king of the north will come against him with chariots, and with horsemen, and with many ships; and they will enter into the land: and he will break in pieces, and pass on: 
\v 41 and he will enter into the land of beauty, and many will fail: but these will escape out of his hand, Edom, and, Moab, and the chief of the children of Ammon. 
\v 42 He will stretch forth \add his\add* hand over the land; and the land of Egypt will not escape. 
\v 43 He will have the mastery over the secret \add treasures\add* of gold and silver, and over all the desirable \add possessions\add* of Egypt, and of the Libyans and Ethiopians in their strongholds. 
\v 44 But rumours and anxieties out of the east and from the north will trouble him; and he will come with great wrath to destroy many. 
\v 45 He will pitch the tabernacle of his palace between the seas in the holy mountain of beauty: \add but\add* he will come to his portion, and there is no one to deliver him. 
\c 12 
\p
\v 1 At that time Michael the great prince will stand up, that stands over the children of your people: and there will be a time of tribulation, such tribulation as has not been from the time that there was a nation on the earth until that time: at that time your people will be delivered, \add even\add* every one that is written in the book. 
\v 2 Many of those who sleep in the dust of the earth will awake, some to everlasting life, and some to reproach and everlasting shame. 
\v 3 The wise will shine as the brightness of the firmament, and \add some\add* of the many righteous as the stars forever and ever. 
\p
\v 4 You, Daniel, close the words, and seal the book to the time of the end; until many are taught, and knowledge is increased. 
\p
\v 5 I Daniel saw, and, behold, two others stood, one on one side of the bank of the river, and the other on the other side of the bank of the river. 
\v 6 And \add one\add* said to the man clothed in linen, who was over the water of the river, When \add will be\add* the end of the wonders which you have mentioned? 
\v 7 I heard the man clothed in linen, who was over the water of the river, and he lifted up his right hand and his left hand to heaven, and swore by him that lives forever, that \add it should be\add* for a time of times and half a time: when the dispersion is ended they will know all these things. 
\p
\v 8 I heard, but I didn't understand: and I said, O Lord, what \add will be\add* the end of these things? 
\v 9 He said, Go, Daniel: for the words are closed and sealed up to the time of the end. 
\v 10 Many must be tested, and thoroughly whitened, and tried with fire, and sanctified; but the transgressors will transgress: and none of the transgressors will understand; but the wise will understand. 
\v 11 From the time of the removal of the perpetual sacrifice, when the abomination of desolation will be set up, \add there will be\add* a thousand two hundred and ninety days. 
\v 12 Blessed is he who waits, and comes to the thousand three hundred and thirty-five days. 
\v 13 But go, and rest; for \add there are\add* yet days and seasons to the fulfilment of the end; and you will stand in your lot at the end of the days. 
